    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Al-Mansur commissioned Baghdad on 30 July 762 as the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, on a site that had been settled since at least the Neo-Babylonian period.</li><li>For two centuries it was one of the largest cities in the world and a centre of translation, science, law and trade, until political divisions and the loss of provinces slowed its growth.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under Hulegu sacked it in 1258 and ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> held it before it became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> in 1921.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Bricks stamped with the name and titles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> have been found along the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> at Baghdad, so the site was in use more than a thousand years before al-Mansur built his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/round-city-of-baghdad">Round City</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

