    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">762</span><span class="rf-text">Al-Mansur commissions the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/round-city-of-baghdad">Round City</a> on 30 July</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">9th century</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom">House of Wisdom</a> and the translation movement flourish under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1055</span><span class="rf-text">The Seljuk leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughril">Tughril Beg</a> takes Baghdad from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1258</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under Hulegu capture Baghdad on 10 February and end the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1401</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> sacks Baghdad</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1534</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> take Baghdad and make it the administrative capital of Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1917</span><span class="rf-text">British forces capture Baghdad on 11 March</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1932</span><span class="rf-text">Baghdad becomes the capital of the independent Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii.jpg" alt="Nebuchadnezzar II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nebuchadnezzar_II_crop.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Baghdad lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, on a flat plain of alluvial origin. The site was occupied before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> built there. An extensive facing of Neo-Babylonian brickwork has been identified along the west bank of the river, with bricks stamped with the name and titles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a>, and Assyrian geographical lists record a place with a name very similar to Baghdad, which probably refers to a town on the same ground. A boundary stone from the reign of Merodach-baladan I, in the 12th century BCE, gives the name as Bag-da-du. The origin of the name is disputed. Arab authors looked for it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a>, and the commonest meaning they gave was "bestowed by God"; modern scholars generally favour this reading, a compound of <i>bagh</i>, "god", and <i>dād</i>, "given".</p><p>The Abbasids, after their victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>, wanted a capital of their own. On 30 July 762 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Mansur commissioned a new city north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, the Sasanian capital, with the guidance of the Iranian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barmakids">Barmakids</a>. The site controlled routes along the Tigris and had abundant water. According to al-Tabari, Christian monks had foretold that a lord named Miklas would build a great city there, and al-Mansur had been called Miklas as a child. The astrologers Naubakht, a Zoroastrian, and Mashallah, a Jew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, are said to have chosen the day under the sign of Leo. Al-Mansur named his city Madinat as-Salam, the "City of peace"; by the 11th century Baghdad was almost the only name used.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Baghdad&#8221; cites where it covers Name, Foundation:</p><ul><li>Gastron Wiet, Baghdad: Metropolis of the Abbasid Caliphate (University of Oklahoma Press, 1971)</li><li>Walther Sallaberger, Akkade-Zeit und Ur III-Zeit (Universitätsverlag, 1999)</li><li>Duri, A.A., Bag̲h̲dād (Brill, 2012)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/round-city-of-baghdad"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/round-city-of-baghdad.jpg" alt="Round City of Baghdad" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/round-city-of-baghdad">Round City of Baghdad</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baghdad_150_to_300_AH.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">William Muir, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Within a generation of its founding Baghdad had become a centre of learning and commerce. Its position on the Khurasan road and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> linked it to trade routes towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, and its water supply was plentiful. The city grew so large that it was divided into three judicial districts: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/round-city-of-baghdad">Round City</a>, al-Karkh (al-Sharqiyya) and Askar al-Mahdi. Al-Mansur moved the markets to al-Karkh, away from the Round City, and officials called <i><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhtasib">muhtasib</a></i> checked weights, measures and the quality of goods. A banking system developed, the city's suitability for paper production lowered the cost of books, and libraries and bookshops appeared.</p><p>Baghdad is likely to have been the largest city in the world from shortly after its foundation until the 930s, when it tied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. Estimates of its size vary widely. Several put the population at over a million at its peak, and some at between 1.2 million and 2 million by the 10th century, while others give between 300,000 and 500,000 for the 10th century. Growth slowed as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a>, provinces were lost and other powers dominated the city. After the Mongol sack Baghdad was rebuilt and flourished under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, but never rose to its former standing. In the later 18th century, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> government, it revived somewhat, and in the 1970s a sharp rise in the price of petroleum brought a new period of prosperity, with new sewers, water supplies and highways. Its population grew from an estimated 145,000 in 1900 to 580,000 in 1950.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tughril"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tughril.jpg" alt="Tughril" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tughril">Tughril</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seljuk_Sultan_Tugrul_Bey_sitting_on_his_throne._Topkap%C4%B1_Saray%C4%B1_M%C3%BCzesi_1653-3_(304_B).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1314 artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Baghdad was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> from 762. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a> for a time, from 836 to 892, and from the 10th century the city was dominated by outside powers: the Iranian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> (945 to 1055) and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> (1055 to 1135). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughril">Tughril Beg</a> took the city in 1055 and the Seljuks ruled as sultans in the name of the Abbasid caliphs, whose protector he considered himself. In 1058 the general al-Basasiri, an adherent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismailis</a>, held it for the Fatimid caliph. After the Mongol conquest of 1258 it was ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, then taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> in 1393 and 1401. It then became a provincial capital under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalayirid-sultanate">Jalayirids</a> (1400 to 1411), the Qara Qoyunlu (1411 to 1469), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> (1469 to 1508) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a>, who held it from 1508 or 1509 until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took it in 1534.</p><p>The Safavids retook it in 1624 and Murad IV recaptured it for the Ottomans in 1638. In the later 18th century the city was under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> government, and direct Ottoman rule was reimposed in 1831 under Ali Riza Pasha. The municipality was established in 1868, and in 1869 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midhat-pasha">Midhat Pasha</a> began the reforms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanzimat">Tanzimat</a>. Baghdad was the headquarters of the Sixth Corps of the Ottoman army. British forces captured it on 11 March 1917, and in 1921, under the Mandate, it became the capital of a British-protected monarchy; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> became independent in 1932. The monarchy ended on 14 July 1958, when army units under Abdul-Karim Qasim seized the city and Iraq became a republic.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Baghdad&#8221; cites where it covers Modern era:</p><ul><li>Orit Bashkin, New Babylonians: A History of Jews in Modern Iraq (Stanford University Press, 2012)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom.jpg" alt="House of Wisdom" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom">House of Wisdom</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maqamat_hariri.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zereshk, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/round-city-of-baghdad">Round City</a> had four gates, named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, Khurasan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> after the places they led to. Straight roads ran from the gates to the centre, lined with vaulted arcades of shops, and the heart of the city was kept as a royal preserve. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s Golden Gate Palace stood at the centre under a green dome 48 metres high. Baghdad had night watchmen, lamplighters, health and market inspectors and a police force, and by the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> a few thousand public baths. At night there were taverns, halls for chess and backgammon, plays, concerts and storytellers, whose tales were later told in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-thousand-and-one-nights">Arabian Nights</a>.</p><p>Arabic was the language of science, but the scholars were also <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriacs</a>, Nestorians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Arab Christians. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom">House of Wisdom</a>, a centre for translating works into Arabic, had the largest collection of books in the world by the middle of the 9th century. Scholars in the city included the translator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunayn-ibn-ishaq">Hunayn ibn Ishaq</a>, the mathematician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi">al-Khwarizmi</a> and the philosopher al-Kindi, and al-Jahiz contributed to Mu'tazili theology and al-Tabari to the interpretation of the Qur'an. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a> and Hanbali schools of law drew scholars, and according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> the residents were mostly Hanbalis. In the 19th century Baghdad was a leading centre of Jewish learning; the Jewish population reached 30,000 by 1884 and about 50,000 by 1900, over a quarter of the city's population.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Baghdad&#8221; cites where it covers Religion, Architecture:</p><ul><li>Michael G. Walling, Iraq Full Circle: From Shock and Awe to the Last Combat Patrol in Baghdad and Beyond (Bloomsbury Publishing, 2012)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mongols"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mongols.jpg" alt="Mongols" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_entre_mongols_%26_chinois_(1211).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sayf al-Vâhidî. Hérât. Afghanistan, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Baghdad was besieged in 812 to 813 in the civil war between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-amin">al-Amin</a> and his brother, and al-Amin was killed. It was besieged again in 865. On 10 February 1258 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> led by Hulegu, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, captured the city. Many quarters were ruined by fire, siege or looting, the Mongols killed most of the inhabitants, including the caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mustasim">al-Musta'sim</a>, and the canals and dykes of the irrigation system were destroyed. The sack ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> occupied the city in 1393 and sacked it in 1401, when he is said to have spared almost no one and ordered each soldier to bring two severed heads. When the Safavid army entered in 1624 a massacre occurred. Plague and cholera struck repeatedly, and at times, it is said, two thirds of the population died.</p><p>On 1 and 2 June 1941, after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-ali-al-gaylani">Rashid Ali al-Gaylani</a>'s government, Jewish residents were attacked in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farhud">Farhud</a>. Scholarly accounts commonly give about 180 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> killed; the Iraqi commission reported 128 killed and 210 injured. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran-iraq-war">Iran–Iraq War</a> of the 1980s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> fired missiles at Baghdad and power plants and refineries were damaged. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-war">Gulf War</a> of 1991 coalition bombing damaged infrastructure, and a raid on 13 February killed at least 408 civilians at Amiriya. US forces took the city on 9 April 2003 after heavy bombing, and looting followed. An insurgency and a civil war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and Shia armed groups followed, and a further war lasted from 2013 to 2017.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/one-thousand-and-one-nights"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/one-thousand-and-one-nights.jpg" alt="One Thousand and One Nights" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sughrat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Al-Mubashshir ibn Fatik, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/round-city-of-baghdad">Round City</a> left no visible remains: its walls were gone by 912, and there is no agreement on where it stood. What survives of medieval Baghdad is mostly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rusafa">Rusafa</a> quarter. The Mustansiriyya <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">Madrasa</a>, founded in 1227 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Mustansir, stands in the Qushla area on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> with Ottoman mosques, and the Baghdad Citadel, built in the 1430s under the Qara Qoyunlu, survived until the 20th century. The tombs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a> Musa al-Kadhim and Muhammad at-Taqi at Kadhimiyya and the mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-hanifa">Abu Hanifa</a> at Adhamiyya remain religious sites. Mutanabbi Street, named after the 10th century poet, is the historic centre of bookselling. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-museum-of-iraq">National Museum of Iraq</a>, built under King Faisal, lost much of its collection to looting during the 2003 invasion.</p><p>Baghdad's reputation as a centre of learning rests on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom">House of Wisdom</a>, the translation movement and the scholars of the 9th and 10th centuries, and many tales of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-thousand-and-one-nights">Arabian Nights</a> are set in the city of that age. It was designated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> City of Literature in December 2015. Today Baghdad is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, divided by the Tigris into Rusafa on the east bank and Karkh on the west.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Baghdad&#8221; cites where it covers Modern era:</p><ul><li>Orit Bashkin, New Babylonians: A History of Jews in Modern Iraq (Stanford University Press, 2012)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Baghdad was founded in 762 by the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Mansur and became a leading city of learning and trade. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> sacked it in 1258, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> in 1401, and it later passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> before becoming the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> in the 20th century.</em></p>

